    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so the LDL is basically the stuff that gets stuck in the arteries... is it like it's actively trying to damage them or is it just a passive buildup? like what is actually happening inside the vessel?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "LDL acts like a sticky trailer dragging excess oil through your arteries, and when it reaches the vessel wall, it gets stuck and nourishes macrophages that turn into \"foam cells,\" which lack structural integrity. These foam cells then die and become a stable but calcifying plaque layer deep inside the vessel wall, slowly expanding over time to narrow the pathway for blood flow. While the LDL itself is chemically passive, its presence creates a chemical inflammation signal that actively recruits immune cells, turning a slow mechanical blockage into an active disease process.",
      "source": "target",
